I feel like memories are flooding back to me of shooting this with Ed, like, pumping the balloon.

1117
00:48:30.000 --> 00:48:31.280
Trying to make these balloons.

1118
00:48:31.700 --> 00:48:33.500
And they were all horrible.

1119
00:48:33.800 --> 00:48:34.240
They were terrible.

1120
00:48:34.340 --> 00:48:35.140
They were terrible.

1121
00:48:35.420 --> 00:48:42.540
So, you guys, if you notice a bunch of weird-looking balloon animals on the conference room table,

1122
00:48:42.540 --> 00:48:44.580
they were Ed Helms' attempts.

1123
00:48:45.620 --> 00:48:47.440
I am so remembering this now.

1124
00:48:47.580 --> 00:48:48.560
We laughed so hard.

1125
00:48:48.740 --> 00:48:54.080
Because I think, you know, the people who do balloon animals, they make it look so easy.

1126
00:48:54.400 --> 00:48:54.500
I know.

1127
00:48:54.680 --> 00:48:56.980
And I think we just all took for granted.

1128
00:48:56.980 --> 00:49:03.560
Like, the writers, Brian as director, Ed, all of us that just, like, he could just make something.

1129
00:49:03.700 --> 00:49:06.780
And we knew, like, oh, they're not going to come out great.

1130
00:49:06.780 --> 00:49:12.040
But, like, this was, like, he couldn't even get them to, like, he couldn't even make an X.

1131
00:49:12.200 --> 00:49:14.940
Like, he could do—they just kept flying everywhere.

1132
00:49:15.040 --> 00:49:15.180
Yeah.

1133
00:49:15.400 --> 00:49:16.240
And also—

1134
00:49:16.240 --> 00:49:16.780
So hard.

1135
00:49:16.900 --> 00:49:19.860
Also, it was way more pumping than I think—

1136
00:49:19.860 --> 00:49:21.400
There was so much pumping time.

1137
00:49:21.820 --> 00:49:24.200
There was so much time spent pumping the balloons.

1138
00:49:24.380 --> 00:49:25.120
Oh, my gosh.

1139
00:49:25.380 --> 00:49:27.140
What a fun thing to remember.

1140
00:49:27.320 --> 00:49:28.060
Oh, my gosh.

1141
00:49:28.060 --> 00:49:29.800
I was cracking up.

1142
00:49:29.880 --> 00:49:32.280
And, you know, part of it's in deleted scenes.

1143
00:49:32.280 --> 00:49:35.820
But then you see the balloons on the conference table in later scenes.

1144
00:49:36.000 --> 00:49:36.860
They're so awful.

1145
00:49:36.960 --> 00:49:37.560
They're awful.

1146
00:49:37.740 --> 00:49:40.540
And this was Andy's way of, like, easing the tension.

1147
00:49:42.000 --> 00:49:43.060
Oh, my goodness.

1148
00:49:43.420 --> 00:49:45.620
Oh, thank you for reminding me of that.

1149
00:49:45.980 --> 00:49:50.960
Well, things might have taken a turn for Tallahassee back in Tallahassee.

1150
00:49:51.000 --> 00:49:54.400
Because now, Packer is doing the cherry stem thing.

1151
00:49:54.580 --> 00:49:54.920
Yes.

1152
00:49:54.920 --> 00:49:56.900
Or he's tying it with his tongue into a knot.

1153
00:49:56.900 --> 00:50:03.780
And this made me laugh so hard when Dwight says, please, anyone can tie a knot.

1154
00:50:03.940 --> 00:50:05.880
The real talent's in untying it.

1155
00:50:06.020 --> 00:50:12.240
And he takes the cherry stem that was in Packer's mouth and puts it in his mouth.

1156
00:50:12.500 --> 00:50:15.880
And then he works real hard to untie it.

1157
00:50:16.100 --> 00:50:20.200
You guys, in deleted scenes, this moment plays out.

1158
00:50:20.500 --> 00:50:23.600
And Rain as Dwight is really struggling.

1159
00:50:23.600 --> 00:50:27.260
Like, he's working so hard on that cherry stem.

1160
00:50:27.440 --> 00:50:32.560
And there is just this awkward silence as Nellie and Packer watch Dwight.

1161
00:50:32.880 --> 00:50:36.540
And then ultimately, he unknotts it.

1162
00:50:36.620 --> 00:50:38.260
And he spits it out.

1163
00:50:38.480 --> 00:50:39.840
And then he holds it up.

1164
00:50:39.900 --> 00:50:43.180
And he goes, there it is, straight enough to pick your teeth with.

1165
00:50:43.380 --> 00:50:44.940
He's, like, handing it to Nellie.

1166
00:50:45.040 --> 00:50:46.380
It's so disgusting.

1167
00:50:46.940 --> 00:50:48.160
Oh, my gosh.

1168
00:50:48.300 --> 00:50:51.520
This is our second cherry stem reference on The Office.

1169
00:50:51.520 --> 00:50:52.640
Remember Ben Franklin?

1170
00:50:52.640 --> 00:50:53.880
Oh, yeah.

1171
00:50:54.380 --> 00:50:54.600
Was.

1172
00:50:54.780 --> 00:50:55.260
Was.

1173
00:50:55.600 --> 00:50:55.660
Was.

1174
00:50:55.660 --> 00:50:56.420
Cherry stem.

1175
00:50:56.700 --> 00:50:58.180
Was an expert with the cherry stem.

1176
00:50:58.920 --> 00:51:04.320
Well, also in Tallahassee, Erin and Ryan have sneaked into the hotel kitchen to make a waffle.

1177
00:51:05.040 --> 00:51:09.520
And I'll have you know that we shot these kitchen scenes in the actual hotel kitchens.

1178
00:51:09.880 --> 00:51:10.940
It looked real.

1179
00:51:11.180 --> 00:51:11.460
Yes.

1180
00:51:11.700 --> 00:51:13.680
In the morning, we took over the hotel bar.

1181
00:51:13.840 --> 00:51:15.600
And in the afternoon, we took over the kitchen.

1182
00:51:16.380 --> 00:51:21.080
And, you know, I'm always fascinated by the inner workings of the production.

1183
00:51:21.280 --> 00:51:22.340
Like, how does this work?

1184
00:51:22.480 --> 00:51:23.920
And I asked Steve Burgess.

1185
00:51:24.060 --> 00:51:28.440
And he said, you know, we shot at this hotel over two different weeks for two different episodes.

1186
00:51:29.120 --> 00:51:31.360
He said it was like a whole ordeal.

1187
00:51:31.600 --> 00:51:33.520
Like, we took over their parking lot.

1188
00:51:33.620 --> 00:51:35.620
We took over the whole second floor of the hotel.

1189
00:51:35.840 --> 00:51:39.180
On various days, we were in the lobby or the bar or the kitchen.

1190
00:51:39.580 --> 00:51:41.420
During the week, we kept all our stuff there.

1191
00:51:41.420 --> 00:51:44.480
They had guards that would guard everything like 24 hours a day.

1192
00:51:44.600 --> 00:51:47.720
But we would move it all back to our stages for the weekend.

1193
00:51:48.240 --> 00:51:50.380
Because it wasn't just the stuff in the hotel either.

1194
00:51:50.560 --> 00:51:53.460
Like, in the parking lot were all the crew trucks and all the trailers.

1195
00:51:53.460 --> 00:51:58.180
And where we would eat lunch, you know, that was like where you would have catering for lunch.

1196
00:51:58.280 --> 00:51:59.280
It's like a whole thing.

1197
00:51:59.600 --> 00:52:01.660
Logistically, that is so much work.

1198
00:52:01.780 --> 00:52:06.020
Like, it's like every week you completely move into your house and then you move out.

1199
00:52:06.240 --> 00:52:06.520
Yes.

1200
00:52:06.640 --> 00:52:07.360
Imagine that.

1201
00:52:07.540 --> 00:52:07.840
Yes.

1202
00:52:08.180 --> 00:52:08.580
Exactly.

1203
00:52:09.100 --> 00:52:11.040
People working around the clock to make that happen.

1204
00:52:11.420 --> 00:52:12.140
Just amazing.

1205
00:52:12.520 --> 00:52:17.080
So also in this scene, Aaron says, oh, wow, they left the lights on.

1206
00:52:17.200 --> 00:52:21.580
And Ryan says that they leave the lights on because it's less expensive than turning them on and off.

1207
00:52:21.720 --> 00:52:24.800
And Aaron is really impressed with how smart he is.

1208
00:52:24.940 --> 00:52:25.120
Yeah.

1209
00:52:25.500 --> 00:52:25.800
You know.

1210
00:52:26.420 --> 00:52:28.180
Well, I decided to check this out.

1211
00:52:28.420 --> 00:52:29.840
I wanted to fact check Ryan.

1212
00:52:30.080 --> 00:52:30.720
Is this true?

1213
00:52:30.720 --> 00:52:36.960
Because, you know, sometimes you drive through a city late at night and you see these big, tall business buildings.

1214
00:52:37.540 --> 00:52:42.400
And you know no one's in them, really, because it's nighttime and all the lights are on.

1215
00:52:42.400 --> 00:52:53.480
Well, according to energy.gov, the cost effectiveness of when to turn the lights on or off depends on the kind of bulb and the cost of electricity.

1216
00:52:53.480 --> 00:52:57.280
So light bulbs have a kind of operating life.

1217
00:52:57.420 --> 00:53:01.140
And that can be affected by how many times they're turned on and off.

1218
00:53:01.280 --> 00:53:05.360
So the more often they're switched on and off, the lower their operating life.

1219
00:53:05.560 --> 00:53:07.720
But I can get more specific with you.

1220
00:53:08.600 --> 00:53:15.220
Incandescent lighting should definitely be turned off if you're not using it because they're the least efficient kind of lighting.

1221
00:53:15.220 --> 00:53:16.580
They give off a lot of heat.

1222
00:53:16.740 --> 00:53:19.400
You'll keep your room cooler by turning them off.

1223
00:53:19.540 --> 00:53:21.580
And you'll use less electricity.

1224
00:53:22.300 --> 00:53:26.860
Halogen lighting is slightly more efficient but should also be turned off when not in use.

1225
00:53:27.880 --> 00:53:34.720
However, fluorescent lighting is the light where you shorten the light span if you turn it on and off.

1226
00:53:35.020 --> 00:53:40.400
And there's a popular belief that these lights use a lot of energy to get started, which is true.

1227
00:53:40.420 --> 00:53:42.340
You know, when you turn on fluorescent lights, it's like.

1228
00:53:43.780 --> 00:53:44.280
Yeah.

1229
00:53:44.280 --> 00:53:48.600
And it is better to not turn them off for short periods of time.

1230
00:53:48.940 --> 00:53:51.740
But you could turn them off for like all night.

1231
00:53:51.960 --> 00:53:57.400
But if you're just leaving this hotel kitchen for like 30 minutes, just leave them on.

1232
00:53:57.820 --> 00:54:04.680
In short, it's not about the cost of electricity that you save, but on the replacement coSt.

1233
00:54:04.900 --> 00:54:07.520
Like you'd have to replace those fluorescent lights more often.

1234
00:54:07.760 --> 00:54:10.280
So I guess Ryan's right.

1235
00:54:10.540 --> 00:54:14.120
Because then your carbon footprint is building more lights.

1236
00:54:14.700 --> 00:54:15.960
Using more materials.

1237
00:54:16.280 --> 00:54:18.300
Using the machinery to make the lights.

1238
00:54:18.960 --> 00:54:23.280
And the cost of having to buy all the light bulbs and the labor of putting them in.

1239
00:54:23.560 --> 00:54:23.780
Yeah.

1240
00:54:24.180 --> 00:54:25.600
So I guess Ryan's right.

1241
00:54:25.600 --> 00:54:26.240
I was annoyed.

1242
00:54:26.380 --> 00:54:27.340
I wanted Ryan to be wrong.

1243
00:54:28.120 --> 00:54:30.980
Ryan is working so hard to get laid.

1244
00:54:31.160 --> 00:54:31.920
My gosh.

1245
00:54:32.740 --> 00:54:34.020
He's really working hard.

1246
00:54:34.780 --> 00:54:35.820
It made me cringe.

1247
00:54:36.080 --> 00:54:36.960
It did me too.

1248
00:54:40.690 --> 00:54:43.770
So listen, all of a sudden though, people are going to come in this kitchen.

1249
00:54:44.190 --> 00:54:50.090
And I guess what they decide to do, rather than just act like, oh my gosh, sorry, we didn't mean to be in here.

1250
00:54:50.090 --> 00:54:54.370
We got turned around, which is what I would do if I was caught sneaking into a kitchen.

1251
00:54:55.470 --> 00:55:02.270
They decide to hide in the kitchen, a la Jurassic Park, kind of like under the table.

1252
00:55:02.710 --> 00:55:03.450
From the raptors.

1253
00:55:03.570 --> 00:55:04.750
Yeah, that's what it reminded me of.

1254
00:55:04.970 --> 00:55:10.270
You know what's a great thing to do if you're somewhere and you maybe shouldn't be in that part of a building?

1255
00:55:10.410 --> 00:55:10.990
Okay, what?

1256
00:55:11.190 --> 00:55:12.130
If you're discovered.

1257
00:55:12.430 --> 00:55:12.730
What?

1258
00:55:13.310 --> 00:55:14.630
Just say, bathroom?

1259
00:55:14.990 --> 00:55:17.610
Why do we have so many excuses?

1260
00:55:17.950 --> 00:55:25.130
Why are we so ready to get out of a situation where we're in a part of a building we shouldn't be in?

1261
00:55:25.170 --> 00:55:26.790
Have we done this a lot in our life?

1262
00:55:26.890 --> 00:55:27.130
Why?

1263
00:55:27.350 --> 00:55:30.050
I think we're both like snoops.

